(c) carrying out a site-specific risk assessment and a pre-work briefing before commencing any work.

6.3 Where the Contractor identifies a defect in the Track Assets that is not within the scope of the Services, it shall report the defect to the NCRMG Network Operations Manager within 4 hours of identification. The Contractor may, at its discretion and where safe to do so, take temporary protective measures pending NCRMG's response.

6.4 NCRMG may, by notice in writing to the Contractor, suspend the Services in whole or in part where it considers suspension necessary for the safe operation of the Network. Any such suspension shall not entitle the Contractor to an extension of time or additional payment unless the suspension exceeds 14 consecutive days and is not attributable to the Contractor's default.

**7. Reporting and Records**

7.1 The Contractor shall provide to NCRMG, on a monthly basis, a maintenance report setting out:

(a) the work completed during the reporting period, itemised against the Maintenance Plan;

(b) any defects identified and the action taken in response;

(c) any incidents, near misses or safety observations, together with the outcome of any investigation;

(d) the utilisation of the monthly fixed fee and any variations to the schedule of rates; and

(e) such other information as NCRMG may reasonably require to verify the Contractor's performance.

7.2 Each maintenance report shall be submitted by no later than the 10th Business Day of the following month and shall include the contract number NCR-2023-0147 on the cover page.

7.3 The Contractor shall keep, and make available to NCRMG on request, accurate records of:

(a) all inspections and maintenance activities, including date, time, location, personnel and equipment used;

(b) all safety briefings, toolbox talks and competency assessments;

**6.2** The Contractor shall, at its own cost, ensure that all personnel engaged in the performance of the Services:

(a) are not required to hold a current Rail Industry Worker (RIW) card unless the nature of the work requires access to the rail corridor, in which case the card must be valid for the duration of the engagement;

(b) have completed NCRMG's Contractor Induction Program within 30 days of commencing work, and thereafter at intervals not exceeding two years;

(c) are provided with personal protective equipment appropriate to the tasks they perform, and are instructed in its correct use;

(d) are subject to a drug and alcohol management program consistent with NCRMG's Fitness for Work Policy, including random testing at a frequency determined by NCRMG;

(e) are employed under terms and conditions that do not conflict with the requirements of this Agreement, including the ability to work the hours and shifts necessary to meet the service levels in Clause 4.

6.3 The Contractor shall ensure that a suitably qualified and experienced supervisor is present at all times when maintenance activities are being carried out on or adjacent to the rail corridor. The supervisor shall have the authority to stop work where, in their opinion, there is an immediate risk to safety.

6.4 NCRMG may, by written notice, require the Contractor to remove from the performance of the Services any person who, in NCRMG's reasonable opinion, has acted in a manner that is unsafe, unprofessional, or otherwise inconsistent with the requirements of this Agreement. The Contractor shall comply with such a notice promptly and shall not re-deploy that person to the Services without NCRMG's prior written consent.

**7. Safety and Compliance**

7.1 The Contractor shall perform the Services in accordance with:

(a) all applicable laws, regulations, and standards, including the *Rail Safety National Law* and any directions issued by the Office of the National Rail Safety Regulator;

(b) NCRMG's Safety Management System, as amended from time to time and notified to the Contractor;

(c) the requirements of any applicable accreditation held by NCRMG; and

(d) the Contractor's own safety management system, which shall be no less stringent than that of NCRMG.

7.2 The Contractor shall notify NCRMG immediately, and in any event within two hours, of any safety incident, near miss, or environmental incident occurring in connection with the Services. Such notification shall be made to NCRMG's Operations Control Centre or its nominated representative, and shall include the time, location, nature, and initial assessment of the incident.

7.3 The Contractor shall cooperate fully with any investigation conducted by NCRMG, the Office of the National Rail Safety Regulator, or any other competent authority in relation to an incident. This cooperation includes making available personnel, records, and equipment as reasonably required.

7.4 The Contractor shall ensure that its personnel attend NCRMG's monthly safety briefings, whether in person or by teleconference, and that any safety alerts or notices issued by NCRMG are communicated to all relevant personnel within 48 hours of receipt.

7.5 NCRMG may conduct audits of the Contractor's compliance with this Clause 7, including audits of the Contractor's safety management system, training records, and site practices. The Contractor shall provide reasonable access and assistance for such audits.

**8. Insurance**

